8. Legendre Functions (p.1)
Mathematical Properties
Notation
The conventions used are z=x+iy, x, y real, and in particular, x always means a real number in the interval -1\leq r\leq+1 with cos th=x where th is likewise a real number; n and m are positive integers or zero; n and m are unrestricted except where otherwise indicated.
Other notations are:
P^n(x) for n|P_n(x)
Px=(x) for (-1)^mP_n(x)
T_n(x) for (-1)^mP_n(x)
P_n(x)
Px=(x) for (-1)^m\sqrt{(2n+1)(n-m)!}P_n(x)
Px=(x) for P_n(x), Cx=c/x  (Rx>1)
Cx=c/x for e^n+iQx=(z)
Q_n(x) for \sin\frac{(v+u)\pi}{\sin\pi}Q_n(x)
Various other definitions of the functions occur as well as mixing of definitions.
8.1.3 Qg(z)=e^{i\pi^2-v-1/2}\frac{\Gamma(v+\mu+1)}{\Gamma(v+\beta)}z^{-v-\mu-1}(z^2-1)^{ik}F(1+\frac2+\mu,2\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\nu}{2}+\frac{\mu}{2};\nu+\frac{3}{2};\frac{1}{2})\quad(|z|>1)
Alternate Forms
(Additional forms may be obtained by means of the transformation formulas of the hypergeometric function, see [8.1].)
8.1.4 P_n^n(z)=2^n(z^2-1)^{-ik}\left(\frac{F\left(-\frac{\nu}{2}-\frac{\mu}{2}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\nu}{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(1\frac{\nu}{2}-\frac{\mu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(1+\frac{\nu}{2}-\frac{\mu}{2}\right)}-2z\frac{F\left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\nu}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\right)\Gamma\left(-\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\right)}\right)\quad(|z^2|<1)
8.1.5 P_n^n(z)=\frac{2^-v-1}{(z^2-1)^{\mu}\Gamma(-\nu-\mu)}F\left(\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2},\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\nu}{2},\frac{\mu}{2};\nu+\frac{3}{2};z^2-2\right)\quad+\frac{2^{\Gamma}(3+\nu)2^{\pi}\Gamma(1+\nu-\mu)}F\left(-\frac{\nu}{2}\frac{\mu}{2}\frac{1}{2},\frac{\nu}{2};\frac{\mu}{2};\frac{1}{2}-\nu;\frac{\nu}{2}=\infty\right)\quad(|z^2|<1)
8.1.6 e^{-i\nu}Q_n^n(z)=\frac{\Gamma(1+\nu+\mu)\Gamma(-\mu)(z-1)^{ik}(z+1)^{-ik}}{2\Gamma(1+\nu-\mu)}F\left(-\nu,1+\nu;1+\mu;\frac{1-\mu}{2}\right)\quad(|z^2|<2)